#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "14c9cf83-8dce-598b-b9cb-ec22966d8fac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12587" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-y^2-x-y$ on the closed region ${D}=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^2:\\,0\\leq x\\leq1,\\: x^2\\leq y\\leq 1 \\big\\}.$ \nYou are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0.5,-0.5),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(0.5,1),$ $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region: \n\n![](images/a4/a45fd135e45e9b40d06c217e9834923ea4f70809a26e020a8d3900e8fa0a0c22.png)\n\nSince the critical point $\\left(0.5,-0.5\\right)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.   \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n\\left(0.5,1\\right), \\quad (0,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.5,1) & ={(0.5)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0.5-1 \\\\  & =0.25-2.5 \\\\  & =-2.25 \\\\ f(0,0) & ={0}^{2}-{0}^{2}-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}-{1}^{2}-0-1 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1,1) & ={1}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2.25$ at the point $\\left(0.5,1\\right),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the point $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "84d1abb1-8b2b-5180-8924-825386924270" :question/math-academy-id "e-12588" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function  $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-4x$ on the closed region $D=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x^2 + y^2 \\leq 4 \\big\\}.$   You are given that the function has a critical point at $(2,0) \\in D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince the point $( 2 , 0 )$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step). \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 2\\cos{t}, 2\\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}-4(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives one solution, $t = \\pi \\in (0,2\\pi).$ But we also have to check the endpoint of the interval, $t=0.$ So, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\pi.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain two candidate points:\n\n$$\n(2,0), \\quad  (-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2) \\\\  & =4+0-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(-2) \\\\  & =4+0+8 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-4$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(-2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124221 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/id #uuid "138b3374-23fe-5705-87ed-e9e27a19e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-12589" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0ce5cde24ada296d945726d9f8e2f0b85900e62e9891f7ba4e25b6c181b076aa.png)\n\n   \n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x^2-2x+y^2+3$ on the closed region\n${D} = \\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\: : \\: x \\geq 0,\\, x^2 + y^2 \\leq4 \\},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol{f}' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/81/815ebf6434a4b776ff59fb59e5b0783162e9ee69c091ae6d0f59ca94e8cf771b.png)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,0).$ Since the point $(1,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 0, t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-2(0)+(t)^{2}+3 \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}+3)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is inside the interval $t \\in (-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on this boundary, we obtain the candidate point $(0,0).$\n\n- The half-circle connecting $(0,-2)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}},t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2}-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (7-2\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives the solution $t=0$ inside the interval $t\\in(-2,2).$  Evaluating the corresponding values of  $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(2,0).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, we obtain a total of four candidates on the boundary:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (0,-2), \\quad (2,0), \\quad (0,2).\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $7$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $2$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $7$ at the points $(0,-2),$  and $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b661fb0-fbd7-5d7c-9691-ecd0f09e25df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282893} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cfcfb7-d24a-59dc-a301-00e471fc1010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283089} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f6b37c-2ea0-56cf-a2f7-1eb401a5865f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283102} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2540ab-61ee-59ba-8715-d4416d7bfad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283331} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ee8fdf-1d2d-5af7-a4f6-e0b7f6b59b0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0cfcfb7-d24a-59dc-a301-00e471fc1010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283089} :answer-field/id #uuid "f238e926-e5b1-5952-bd70-fb1f255a4693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "481b8aaf-ce9a-528b-9903-76b0577c8546" :question/math-academy-id "q-167169" :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=x+y-xy$ on the closed triangular region $D$ with vertices $(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-y & 1-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1-y=0 \\\\ 1-x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ y=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(1,1).$ Since the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =t+0-t(0) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t⟨1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,t) \\\\  & =t+t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,1),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2-2t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=1$ does not belong to the range $t\\in (0,1),$ we do not include the corresponding point in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n- The line connecting $(1,0)$ and $(1,1)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(1,t) \\\\  & =1+t-t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total, we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(1,0),\\,(1,1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(1,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,0)$ and $(1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93c8c1df-9d34-506c-a162-63f4dc34ac81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{min} =-50$ and $f_{max} = 20$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122799} {:answer/id #uuid "08e29d0a-dfee-55a5-be4d-5ac689b1ca2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{min} = 4$ and $f_{max} = 6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122800} {:answer/id #uuid "230b3edb-8f7e-55e8-9b01-910196f5cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{min} =-50$ and $f_{max} = 4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122801} {:answer/id #uuid "678ec8d6-a00f-5ca4-9c0f-2b5c035c1955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{min} =-50$ and $f_{max} = 6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122802} {:answer/id #uuid "7574974f-00a9-5b84-a1f0-e6e7f7ae4813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{min} =-20$ and $f_{max} = 20$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122803}] :answer-field/id #uuid "78b2ba2b-f79b-5ccd-88fe-1d78549d2f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122798}] :question/id #uuid "fc6c7d4b-ed06-5426-80f2-415a147181e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37149" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x, y) = 2 + 6x - 3y + xy$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y = 2x^{2}$ and $y = 8$. You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(3,-6)$, and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2, 8)$, $(1, 2)$, and $(2, 8)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06940bef-a215-5420-8df8-1b3ac15f920f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373327} {:answer/id #uuid "ca155e95-50cc-5275-acc6-fa54f8093bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-4$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373328} {:answer/id #uuid "662e6c67-053c-5fa1-acb1-c6fef45dcb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=4$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373329} {:answer/id #uuid "bcde84a3-5a11-524f-8852-258641bb7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373330} {:answer/id #uuid "048eff16-8812-5b00-a086-bac6d33306cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcde84a3-5a11-524f-8852-258641bb7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-8$ and ${f}_{max}=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373330} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0d98f6-add4-58fe-abc8-484ea418fdb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f539d9c8-b40f-5e02-8e74-4380e9158356" :question/math-academy-id "q-83491" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0331b5d4c9fa3c823d6596c1ab147b8ee459feb4a4feaed3c614e2639ae77e5.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-y,\\,-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4-y=0 \\\\ -x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$"}]}
